{
 "id": "z06",
 "text_original": "I needed the SEC's company filings inside a query. I wrote one statement, a REST table with the SEC's required header, and a real filing came back as a row in about five seconds. The second read took under two. I had written no connector. We estimate that saved an hour or more. Not everything was that easy. USAspending's API returned valid JSON, and Zetaris read it as a single corrupt record, because the server sends a browser page unless you ask for application JSON. One header fixed it, but we lost time there. Then the moment it clicked. Other agents on our team filed new requests into the payment database while we worked. I re-ran one data-quality rule, and the invalid rows went from two to three. No re-registration, no refresh, no pipeline. Zetaris had read the new Postgres rows in place. That rule is now the sweep Probity runs on every payment run.",
 "text_spoken": "I needed the SEC's company filings inside a query. I wrote one statement, a REST table with the SEC's required header, and a real filing came back as a row in about five seconds. The second read took under two. I had written no connector. We estimate that saved an hour or more. Not everything was that easy. U.S.A. spending's API returned valid JSON, and Zuh-tarris read it as a single corrupt record, because the server sends a browser page unless you ask for application JSON. One header fixed it, but we lost time there. Then the moment it clicked. Other agents on our team filed new requests into the payment database while we worked. I re-ran one data-quality rule, and the invalid rows went from two to three. No re-registration, no refresh, no pipeline. Zuh-tarris had read the new Postgres rows in place. That rule is now the sweep Probity runs on every payment run.",
